## Where the reviewer was not followed as written

- ro-a #30. Q16–Q20 cannot become new headings, and the U+200B lines cannot be deleted, without failing heading levels, block count, or the ZWSP check against the Korean source. The sentences inside those blocks were rewritten.
- ro-a #44. The Difficulty row was not deleted. The English table is `Difficulty | Easy | Difficult | Easy`, the next row is a different question (whether severance is payable), and deleting a row fails the checker. The cells are now `Dificultate pentru angajator | Redusă | Ridicată | Redusă`. Preaviz `Necerut` → `Nu este cerut`. Severance `Cerut` / `Necerut` → `Se datorează` / `Nu se datorează`.
- ro-a #50. The Hangul slugs in the 008 and 009 `url` values are canonical metadata.
- ro-b #41. Merging the one-line clauses in 013 and 015 would change the block count. Wording that could be fixed inside a block was fixed; the line breaks were not.
- `pensie alimentară` was kept for child support (扶養費). The review bundled every `alimente` with food. Bare `alimente` for spousal support and for loss of support was changed to `întreținere`. The 010 food-injury phrase was left.
- 010 `1 ori`. That digit is the statutory 1× multiplier, not the glued “one time” in 002. Dropping it fails the number check.
- 005 “din Coreea” in the banking section. The heading already labels it as Korean practice. It was not rewritten as a worldwide rule.
- 008 “cel mai bun rezultat”. That is a fact about the salesperson in the anecdote. The reviewer’s own rewrite keeps the phrase and only fixes “regiunea centrului” → “centrul Taiwanului”, which was applied.
- R13 (ROC year “anul 112”). Not in R2–R10 for this locale, and the Romanian review did not flag it. The Chinese gloss already carries 年.
- 003 `cauțiune` under art. 193. That is a procedural bond. ro-a #29 only asked for the missing `u`. The systemic note is about a lease deposit, a surety, and a parent-company guarantee; those were changed to `garanție` / `depozit de garanție`.
